<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco Serrano, 1st Duke of la Torre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1810">1810</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco Serrano was a Spanish general and politician who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution-spain">Glorious Revolution</a> of September 1868 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-prim">Juan Prim</a> and Admiral Topete, driving Isabella II into exile. He served as regent until Amadeo I arrived in 1871, and after the Pavía coup of January 1874 he headed the executive of the First Republic until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> restoration that December.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-serrano-1st-duke-of-la-torre.jpg" alt="Francisco Serrano, 1st Duke of la Torre" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nadar%27s_photo_of_Serrano_(Retouched).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francisco Serrano Domínguez was born on 17 December 1810 on the Isla de León, now San Fernando, in the Bay of Cádiz, the son of a general and Liberal, Francisco Serrano y Cuenca. Following his father into the army, he became a cadet in 1822. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-carlist-war">First Carlist War</a> from 1834 to 1839, as aide-de-camp of Espoz y <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> in the armies of Queen Isabella II, he rose from captain to brigadier-general. He was first elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> for Málaga in 1839.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Serrano became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s chief military politicians. In 1841 he helped Espartero overthrow the regency of Maria Christina, and in 1843 made a pronunciamiento against Espartero at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. He assisted Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopoldo-odonnell">Leopoldo O'Donnell</a> in 1854 and 1856, and was captain-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> from 1859 to 1862, the first viceroy there to advocate political and financial reforms. O'Donnell made him Duke of la Torre.</p><p>On 7 July 1868 the government arrested Serrano and other generals and took them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>. Admiral Topete's steamer brought him to Cádiz on 18 September. He signed the manifesto of the revolution with Prim, Topete and others, took command of the revolutionary army and routed Isabella's troops at the bridge of Alcolea. The queen fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and Serrano formed a Provisional Government, becoming regent and prime minister. He accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes">Cortes</a>'s choice of Amadeus I of Savoy as king, though he would have preferred Montpensier.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Serrano opposed the federal republic and conspired against it, failed and went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-pavia-y-rodriguez-de-alburquerque">Manuel Pavía</a> sent for him to lead the coup of 3 January 1874, and Serrano again became president of the executive. In his eleven months in office he gave attention to finance, to renewing relations with other powers and to suppressing revolt, but at the end of December 1874 another pronunciamiento restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in 1876 and sat in the senate as a marshal. He died in Madrid in November 1885, a day after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xii">Alfonso XII</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francisco Serrano, 1st Duke of la Torre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In September 1868 a military rising led by Generals Juan Prim and <b>Francisco Serrano</b> and Admiral Juan Bautista Topete, known as the Glorious Revolution, drove Isabella II into exile.</blockquote><a href="/state/sexenio-democratico">Sexenio Democrático</a></li><li><blockquote>A unitary republic under <b>Serrano</b> followed until General Martínez Campos proclaimed Alfonso XII in December 1874, beginning Restoration Spain.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-first-republic">Spanish First Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Isla de León, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.45,-6.21&amp;year=1858" aria-label="Isla de León on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4394 849 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francisco Serrano, 1st Duke of la Torre"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4844 1119h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4844 1119h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.45,-6.21&amp;year=1858">What was here in 1858? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
